<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="tricetin methylation" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi_pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; The flavone aglycone |FRAME: CPD-12570 tricetin| is found in a number of plants, including important cereal crops such as wheat and maize. It has also been isolated from the pollen of members of myrtaceae |CITS: [12440738]|. In a study on the effect of tricetin on human breast cancer, the resulting cell cycle blockade was attributed to the action of tricetin on G2M phase. Tricetin is thus considered a potential chemotherapeutic drug against human breast cancer |CITS: [19705844]|. |FRAME: CPD-12572 Tricin| is a flavonoid typically distributed in sedges, palms, and grasses, including cereal crops such as rice (|FRAME: TAX-4530 &lt;i&gt;Oryza sativa&lt;/i&gt;|), wheat (|FRAME: TAX-4565 &lt;i&gt;Triticum aestivum&lt;/i&gt;|), barley (|FRAME: TAX-4513 &lt;i&gt;Hordeum vulgare&lt;/i&gt;|), maize (|FRAME: TAX-4577 &lt;i&gt;Zea mays&lt;/i&gt;|), and sorghum (|FRAME: TAX-4558 &lt;i&gt;Sorghum bicolor&lt;/i&gt;|) |CITS: [Zhou10]|. It is found mostly as &lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-linked conjugates that serve as allelochemicals |CITS: [20730995]| and insect deterrents |CITS: [Ling07]|. |FRAME: CPD-12572 Tricin| is also found as a flavonoid monomer in monocot lignin, representing the first non-monolignol monomer involved in lignification |CITS: [25667313]|. Tricin is considered a major candidate to be developed as a nutraceutical because of its antioxidant |CITS: [17350657]|, anticancer |CITS: [17089164]|, anti-inflammatory |CITS: [22705359]|, and cardiovascular-protective properties |CITS: [20006366]|. In this pathway a single &lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-methyltransferase (OMT) catalyzes the methylation of the 3&apos;-hydroxyl group of |FRAME: CPD-12570 tricetin|, forming |FRAME: CPD-12571 selgin|, and the subsequent methylation of the 5&apos;-hydroxyl group of the product to form |FRAME: CPD-12572 tricin|. This activcity is known as |FRAME: EC-2.1.1.175 EC 2.1.1.175, tricin synthase| |CITS: [17943312]|. Note that a different pathway for the production of |FRAME: CPD-12572 tricin| in rice, not via |FRAME: CPD-12570 tricetin|, has been described (see |FRAME: PWY-7995 tricin biosynthesis|). In wheat (|FRAME: TAX-4565 &lt;i&gt;Triticum aestivum&lt;/i&gt;|), a different enzyme, known as |FRAME: EC-2.1.1.169 EC 2.1.1.169, tricetin 3&amp;prime;,4&amp;prime;,5&amp;prime;-&lt;em&gt;O&lt;/em&gt;-trimethyltransferase|, catalyzes both of these reactions and an additional methylation of |FRAME: CPD-12572 tricin|, forming |FRAME: CPD-12572 tricin| |CITS: [16730127]|. The wheat enzyme is a homodimer, and each subunit is capable of all three steps of sequential methylation |CITS: [18397325]|.</Description>
